<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-glutamate degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="GLUTAMATE-DEG1-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In this pathway |FRAME:GLT| is deaminated by an NAD-linked dehydrogenase, forming |FRAME:AMMONIA| and |FRAME:2-KETOGLUTARATE|, which is then fed to the |FRAME:TCA|. The pathway is found in microbes and plants. The deamination of |FRAME: GLT| is a key reaction not only in the degradation of |FRAME: GLT|, but also in the degradation of several other amino acids. |FRAME:ARG|, |FRAME:GLN|, |FRAME:HIS|, and |FRAME:PRO| are all converted to |FRAME:GLT| during their catabolism |CITS[3289795]|. The enzymes catalyzing the deamination of |FRAME: GLT| are the ubiquitous glutamate dehydrogenases (GDHs). GDHs catalyze a reversible reaction, and are involved in both the anabolic function of glutamate biosynthesis and the catabolic function of glutamate utilization, depending on the organism and the conditions. In some organisms, a single enzyme can catalyze both functions under different physiological conditions. Most of the prokaryotic enzymes can use either NAD+ (EC 1.4.1.2) or NADP+ (EC 1.4.1.4) |CITS:[4147773]|. Although the NADP+-specific enzymes are capable of catalyzing a reversible reaction, they are believed to take part only in the anabolic reactions of glutamate biosynthesis, and thus included in |FRAME: GLUTSYNIII-PWY|. In yeast cells the amino group of glutamate and the amide group of glutamine are the source of nitrogen for all other macromolecules |CITS: [1975578]|. In order to provide ammonia for the synthesis of glutamine during growth on glutamate-yielding nitrogen sources, cells degrade glutamate into ammonia. The main pathway for |FRAME: TAX-4932| glutamate degradation is catalyzed by the NAD-dependent glutamate dehydrogenase encoded by GDH2 |CITS:[1975578]|. Plants have multiple forms of GDH enzymes. NAD(H)-specific enzymes are localized in the mitochondria, while NADP(H)-specific enzymes are associated with the chloroplasts |CITS: [Srivastava87][9112779]|. Under severe carbon shortage conditions, plants convert |FRAME: GLT| to |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| via this pathway, also known as the GDH shunt. |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| then enters the |FRAME: TCA| and carbon metabolism. Plant mutants lacking this capability have inhibited growth, especially under stress when carbon metabolism is in intense demand |CITS: [8643469]|. The GDH shunt, together with the ammonia assimilation cycle (see |FRAME: PWY-3282|), play important role in the maintenance of carbon and nitrogen balance in plants. The GDH shunt is also thought to play a role in maintaining the internal glutamate concentration which is remarkably constant in Arabidopsis leaves. some organisms posses a glutamate dehydrogenase that can utilize both NAD and NADP as a cofactor. these enzyme are described in the pathway |FRAME: PWY-5766|.</Description>
